# Architecture & state management

**Primary source:** Google's own Unidirectional Data Flow (UDF) guidance
(`developer.android.com/topic/architecture/ui-layer`,
`.../recommendations`). Google deliberately avoids mandating a named
pattern like "MVI" — the requirement is the principle: **state flows
down, events flow up**, `ViewModel` holds and exposes `StateFlow<UiState>`
and survives configuration changes automatically.

**Community layer:** the "MVI-flavored MVVM" naming convention — a
`UiState` data/sealed class, an `Intent`/`Event` sealed class for inbound
actions, and an optional one-shot `Effect` channel for navigation/snackbar
side effects — layered on top of Google's `ViewModel` for team
consistency. This is not a library; it's a naming convention every
screen follows.

## Default: `ViewModel` + `StateFlow<UiState>` + Intent

```kotlin
data class LoginUiState(
    val email: String = "",
    val password: String = "",
    val isLoading: Boolean = false,
    val errorMessage: String? = null,
)

sealed interface LoginIntent {
    data class EmailChanged(val value: String) : LoginIntent
    data class PasswordChanged(val value: String) : LoginIntent
    data object Submit : LoginIntent
}

@HiltViewModel
class LoginViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val authRepository: AuthRepository,
) : ViewModel() {
    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(LoginUiState())
    val uiState: StateFlow<LoginUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()

    fun onIntent(intent: LoginIntent) {
        when (intent) {
            is LoginIntent.EmailChanged -> _uiState.update { it.copy(email = intent.value) }
            is LoginIntent.PasswordChanged -> _uiState.update { it.copy(password = intent.value) }
            LoginIntent.Submit -> submit()
        }
    }

    private fun submit() {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            _uiState.update { it.copy(isLoading = true, errorMessage = null) }
            authRepository.login(_uiState.value.email, _uiState.value.password)
                .onSuccess { _uiState.update { it.copy(isLoading = false) } }
                .onFailure { _uiState.update { it.copy(isLoading = false, errorMessage = it.toString()) } }
        }
    }
}
```

- **`ViewModel`s own presentation state and orchestration**, not business
  logic that belongs in a `core` service (`AuthRepository`, `ApiClient`).
- **A single `Intent` entry point** (`onIntent`) rather than the UI calling
  many separate `viewModel.doThing()` functions directly — makes logging
  and debugging "what is happening right now" drastically easier.
- **Composables own layout**, not state derivation — the `ViewModel`
  decides what the `UiState` looks like; the composable just renders it.

## One-shot events

Per Google's own explicit guidance
(`developer.android.com/topic/architecture/ui-layer/events`): reduce
one-off `ViewModel` events (navigation, snackbar) to a `UiState` field
rather than a `Channel`/`SharedFlow` the UI might miss if it outlives the
producer. If a one-shot channel is genuinely needed, document why in the
screen's spec.

## Use `ViewModel`s at screen level only

- Use `ViewModel` in screen-level composables or navigation
  destinations/graphs — **not** in reusable pieces of UI.
- For complex, dynamic composables that need their own state scope, use
  `rememberViewModelStoreOwner()` rather than reaching for a screen-level
  `ViewModel`.
- **Do not use `AndroidViewModel`.** Use the plain `ViewModel` class; move
  any dependency on `Application`/`Context` to `core` services injected
  via Hilt instead.

## When UDF-as-written is not enough (ADR-gated)

Adopt a strict MVI library (Orbit, Mobius) only when a specific screen's
product spec requires deterministic, exhaustively-testable state
transitions (e.g. a multi-step wizard with undo/redo) — and only after an
ADR per `android-adr.mdc` trigger #1. Do not adopt one wholesale for an
entire app "for consistency" without that trigger.

| Pattern | Default? | Trigger to adopt |
|---|---|---|
| `ViewModel` + `StateFlow<UiState>` + Intent | Yes | N/A — starting point |
| Strict MVI library (Orbit, Mobius) | No | A specific screen's state machine must be exactly reproducible/testable |

## Anti-patterns

- **Business logic, networking calls, or formatting logic inline in a
  composable** — extract to the `ViewModel` or a `core` service.
- **`ViewModel` events exposed via `Channel`/`SharedFlow`** for anything
  that should just be `UiState` — see the one-shot events section above.
- **Calling multiple `viewModel.fetchX()`/`viewModel.doY()` methods
  directly from the UI** instead of a single `onIntent` entry point.

